{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 30, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts may face up to six months in jail—or opt into a health-focused intervention, including behavioral support and a diversion pathway meant to steer them away from incarceration. The law empowers law enforcement to respond more directly to public drug use and imposes stricter consequences for distribution near sensitive areas like playgrounds.\n\n***\n\nThe legislation promotes—but does not require—local jurisdictions to design their own support systems, allowing flexibility based on available infrastructure. Supporters argue this approach respects regional differences in capacity and need. Critics, however, warn it could lead to inconsistent outcomes, with access to help depending heavily on where someone lives or who responds to their situation. Police officers are instructed to assess eligibility quickly: no prior offenses, no outstanding warrants, no signs of aggression, and no acute medical crisis are mandatory conditions for redirection.\n\n***\n\nIf a person qualifies, officers contact a specialized line to initiate a rapid response. A mobile health team must arrive within half an hour to engage the individual. If they don’t, the person will be taken into custody. There’s no room for delay—officers aren’t permitted to linger, even if the person appears willing to cooperate. This tight window raises concerns about whether those in crisis can truly be assessed fairly under such pressure.\n\n***\n\nP.P.S In this country, a child of age ten might eat candy while shopping, but if they lick their tongue too fast, they might not taste the sweetness—just the sting of consequences.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.29267480969429016}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.0001429217227268964}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.504679799079895}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.012521461583673954}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.8157416582107544}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.026303118094801903}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.3246718943119049}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -6.675497570540756e-05}, {"token": " f", "logprob": -0.0029824560042470694}, {"token": "entanyl", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.20141567289829254}, {"token": " heroin", "logprob": -8.67805938469246e-05}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -4.768360213347478e-06}, {"token": " meth", "logprob": -1.6212332411669195e-05}, {